These are different ways to create a thread:

1. Extending Thread:

class MyThread extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
    }
}

Use when: You specifically need to extend/customize Thread. Less preferred in modern Java because Java supports single inheritance.

Explanation:

Customize means change or add your own behavior to the Thread class.

For example:

class MyThread extends Thread {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("My special task");
    }
}

Here you customize the thread's behavior by providing your own run() method.
--------------------------------
2. Implementing Runnable:

class MyTask implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        MyTask task = new MyTask();

        Thread t = new Thread(task);
        t.start();
    }
}

Use when: You want to separate the task from the thread. Generally preferred over extending Thread.

Explanation:

Runnable → defines the task that should be performed
Thread → runs/executes that task

Example:

Runnable task = () -> {
    System.out.println("Doing work");
};

Thread t = new Thread(task);
t.start();
----------------------------
3. Runnable using Lambda: Because Runnable is a functional interface:  {Lambda → implementation of Runnable.run()}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Runnable task = () -> {
            System.out.println("Thread running");
        };

        Thread t = new Thread(task);
        t.start();
    }
}

Or shorter:

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Thread running");
});

t.start();

Use when: The task is small/simple. This is very common.

Explanation:

Using Runnable with Lambda

Use when: The task is small/simple, and you don't want to write a separate Runnable class.

Example:

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Doing work");
});

t.start();

Here:

() -> { ... } → defines the task
Thread → executes the task
start() → starts the thread

Why is it simple?

Without lambda, you would write:

Thread t = new Thread(new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Doing work");
    }
});

With lambda:

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Doing work");
});

So: Lambda is a shorter and cleaner way to implement Runnable, especially for small/simple tasks.

You have two choices with a lambda:

1. Write the task directly in the lambda:

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Doing work");
});

t.start();

Here the task is written directly inside the lambda.

2. Call a separate method from the lambda:

You can keep the actual work in a separate method:

public class Main {

    static void doWork() {
        System.out.println("Doing work");
        System.out.println("Calculating...");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
            doWork();
        });

        t.start();
    }
}
-------------------
4. Anonymous Class extending Thread:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread t = new Thread() {

            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Thread running");
            }
        };

        t.start();
    }
}

Use when: You need a one-time Thread subclass without creating a separate class. Mostly useful for understanding older Java code.

Explanation: Anonymous Class using Thread
Use when: Anonymous Thread class is useful for creating a Thread object with custom behavior for a one-time/specific use,
without creating a separate named class.

Thread t = new Thread() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Doing work");
    }
};

new Thread() → creates a Thread object
{ ... } → gives that Thread object a custom run() implementation
t.start() → starts that thread
----------------------
5. Anonymous Class implementing Runnable:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Runnable task = new Runnable() {

            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Thread running");
            }
        };

        Thread t = new Thread(task);
        t.start();
    }
}

Use when: You need a one-time Runnable task and don't want to create a separate class. Lambda is usually cleaner now.
---------------------
Yes, you can call your own method from the lambda. But you don't write run() inside the lambda because the lambda itself represents the run() method.

Runnable task = () -> {
    doWork();
};
----------------------------
